01:11:10.675 --> 01:11:14.783
a code. It's actually a code that I did draft from scratch in Saratoga

01:11:15.283 --> 01:11:18.757
Springs. I did consult with some artificial turf suppliers in

01:11:19.257 --> 01:11:22.734
the valley. 'Cause my knowledge of turf specifics are

01:11:22.814 --> 01:11:26.380
pretty minimal. And so I did consult with

01:11:27.376 --> 01:11:29.834
a company there, I believe they're in Draper, if I remember right,

01:11:30.605 --> 01:11:34.026
to get the specifics. So that was then incorporated into the code.

01:11:34.701 --> 01:11:38.315
And also the 1760.100, as I was doing the turf,

01:11:39.038 --> 01:11:42.234
this came up. So I'm like, well, let's look at that as well. So we'll

01:11:42.734 --> 01:11:44.804
look at the turf code first here. Okay.

01:11:50.513 --> 01:11:54.121
So yeah, so obviously everything in red, this is all brand new. Uh,

01:11:54.506 --> 01:11:56.558
uh, Section 80 or, uh,

01:11:57.264 --> 01:12:00.984
subsection paragraph 85 is new. Uh, so basically,

01:12:01.321 --> 01:12:04.624
um, we want a no-fade warranty. Obviously things that do

01:12:05.124 --> 01:12:08.600
sit out in the sun have a tendency to fade. Obviously this is a man-made

01:12:09.194 --> 01:12:12.689
product, it's going to be in the sun, uh, if used, uh, quite extensively.

01:12:12.882 --> 01:12:15.426
so we want to have a no-fade warranty on that.

01:12:15.958 --> 01:12:19.549
Multicolor blend to at least mimic real grass.

01:12:19.710 --> 01:12:23.400
Obviously real grass comes in different shades, different hues of green. Could be some,

01:12:23.997 --> 01:12:27.467
you know, depending on your seed mix of your grass, could be, you know,

01:12:28.420 --> 01:12:31.486
one could go dormant at some point, whatever. You know, there's a variety of

01:12:31.503 --> 01:12:34.283
colors typically in grass.

01:12:35.527 --> 01:12:37.289
Of course, put in by manufacturer instructions.

01:12:38.676 --> 01:12:42.272
Pile height, so length of the blade, minimum of 1.5

01:12:42.385 --> 01:12:45.820
inches. So, and 50 ounces, so every

01:12:46.320 --> 01:12:49.497
square yard of turf would be at least 50 ounces in

01:12:49.593 --> 01:12:52.981
weight. And then water permeability of 19

01:12:53.481 --> 01:12:57.524
inches per hour. We'd like to see if you're putting it out there around trees,

01:12:57.636 --> 01:12:59.852
at least give a 2.5-foot clearance around trees.

01:13:00.847 --> 01:13:04.363
Then of course within that you could put wood chips or whatever, but we don't

01:13:04.863 --> 01:13:08.486
want to see turf You know, abutted right up next to tree

01:13:08.986 --> 01:13:12.426
trunks. When they put turf down,

01:13:12.459 --> 01:13:16.052
they put an infill in there. Now, if you ever watch professional

01:13:16.085 --> 01:13:19.339
football or college football and you see somebody drags their foot across the

01:13:19.839 --> 01:13:23.083
turf, you see all the black, that's rubber infill.

01:13:23.680 --> 01:13:27.231
We don't want to see rubber infill. One, you know, you could probably have a

01:13:27.731 --> 01:13:30.152
good argument of being the, you know, is it good for the environment? No,

01:13:30.298 --> 01:13:34.478
probably not. But there are organic materials that can

01:13:34.494 --> 01:13:37.962
be used. It helps support the turf, it helps support the blades,

01:13:38.653 --> 01:13:42.120
it helps with the water drainage as well. Typically it's like

01:13:42.620 --> 01:13:46.391
quartz. There could be some crushed, some type

01:13:46.891 --> 01:13:50.870
of shell, nutshell or something they can use as well, but they then

01:13:51.370 --> 01:13:55.253
put that in once the turf is installed.

01:13:57.116 --> 01:14:00.828
So artificial turf is not gonna be considered to include

01:14:01.328 --> 01:14:02.563
artificial trees, shrubs, or bushes.

01:14:05.311 --> 01:14:08.086
We do need to make sure that stormwater is maintained on site.

01:14:09.267 --> 01:14:13.133
Obviously, if you've got grass or other living material, there's gonna be a difference of

01:14:13.554 --> 01:14:15.966
what happens to stormwater at times,

01:14:17.461 --> 01:14:20.875
less to soak into. We're not gonna— we don't wanna consider

01:14:21.375 --> 01:14:24.941
artificial turf as landscaping. And the minimum living plant

01:14:24.989 --> 01:14:29.069
coverage still applies. So your front yard, you still have to have 50% coverage

01:14:29.569 --> 01:14:33.630
of living plant material, and tree canopies do not count towards

01:14:33.646 --> 01:14:38.159
that 50%. So someone cannot just come in and do their whole front

01:14:38.659 --> 01:14:41.998
yard and park strip as artificial turf. You still have to have 50% of that

01:14:42.498 --> 01:14:44.954
area would still have to have living plant material,

01:14:45.692 --> 01:14:49.065
and any other landscaping standards that may apply would still, would still be applicable.

01:14:51.971 --> 01:14:55.430
And then the next section, state code prohibits the city

01:14:55.930 --> 01:14:59.569
from enforcing or having any rear yard landscape requirements.

01:14:59.795 --> 01:15:03.286
So maybe wise just to take that out of the code.

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:07.918
And I mentioned CC&amp;Rs. We don't do anything with CC&amp;Rs,

01:15:08.063 --> 01:15:11.466
and so we want to remove any reference to that. That's strictly an HOA

01:15:12.547 --> 01:15:16.087
item there, and so that's Those

01:15:16.104 --> 01:15:18.343
are the 2 code amendments we present to you tonight.

